%\VignetteIndexEntry{Comparisons in constrained ordination}
\documentclass[a4paper,10pt]{article}
\usepackage{vegan}

\title{Comparisons in constrained ordination}
\author{Vegan development team}

\date{\footnotesize{
  processed with vegan
\Sexpr{packageDescription("vegan", field="Version")}
in \Sexpr{R.version.string} on \today}}

\begin{document}

\SweaveOpts{strip.white=true}
<<echo=false>>=
options(width = 72, digits = 4)
options("prompt" = "> ", "continue" = "  ")
@

\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
  Function \code{comparisons} asks focused questions about a factor in
  a fitted constrained ordination model. It provides common sets of
  comparisons, user-written comparisons, comparisons within
  interactions, joint tests, multiplicity adjustments, and control
  over the population to which a comparison applies. Each question is
  tested using additional constrained inertia and vegan's permutation
  pseudo-$F$ statistic. This document introduces the interface without
  assuming knowledge of contrast algebra. A technical description of
  the implementation is given in the appendix.
\end{abstract}

\tableofcontents

\section{What is a comparison?}

An overall permutation test can tell us whether a factor is associated
with multivariate composition. It does not answer more focused
questions such as ``does management type HF differ from BF?'', ``does
each treatment differ from the control?'', or ``does the treatment
difference change between habitats?'' Function \code{comparisons}
asks these questions after a model has been fitted with \code{rda},
\code{cca}, or \code{dbrda}.

The function changes the focal factor to the levels named in a
comparison while retaining the rest of the fitted model. This matters
when a model contains covariates, interactions, or transformed terms.
The comparison is then tested with the same kind of permutation
pseudo-$F$ statistic used elsewhere in vegan. It is not a test of an
individual model coefficient, and its result does not depend on the
contrast coding used to fit the factor.

The returned inertia, $R^2$, and pseudo-$F$ measure the magnitude of a
difference, but not its direction. Consequently, reversing a
comparison gives the same test. Inspect the community data, fitted
values, or ordination scores when the ecological direction of a
difference is also needed.

All examples use only 99 permutations to keep this vignette quick to
build. More permutations should normally be used in an analysis. With
99 permutations, the smallest attainable permutation $p$-value is
0.01.

\section{A first example}

We first fit a redundancy analysis in which plant community composition
is related to management and the numeric variable \code{A1}.

<<first-model>>=
library(vegan)
data(dune)
data(dune.env)

mod <- rda(dune ~ Management + A1, data = dune.env)
set.seed(2718)
perm <- shuffleSet(nrow(dune), nset = 99)
@

The default is every pairwise comparison between levels of the named
factor.

<<first-pairwise>>=
pw <- comparisons(mod, "Management", permutations = perm,
                  parallel = 1)
pw
@

Each row is a separate test. The columns have the following meanings:

\begin{itemize}
  \item \code{Comparison} identifies the factor levels being compared.
  \item \code{Df} is the number of independent dimensions in the
    hypothesis. An ordinary pairwise comparison has one degree of
    freedom.
  \item \code{Inertia} is the variation attributed to this comparison,
    after allowing for everything permitted by its null model.
  \item \code{R2} is that inertia as a proportion of the total inertia
    in the response.
  \item \code{F} is the observed pseudo-$F$ statistic.
  \item \code{N.Perm} and \code{Pr(>F)} give the number of permutations
    and the permutation $p$-value.
\end{itemize}

The tests share the supplied permutation matrix, which makes an analysis
reproducible. They nevertheless have different null models and should
be regarded as separate tests.

\section{Ready-made sets of comparisons}

The \code{comparison} argument selects a ready-made recipe. Table
\ref{tab:recipes} summarizes the available choices. The order of factor
levels is important for consecutive, reference, Helmert, and polynomial
comparisons; inspect it with \code{levels} before using those recipes.

\begin{table}[ht]
\centering
\begin{tabular}{ll}
\toprule
Value & Question represented \\
\midrule
\code{"pairwise"} & Every pair of levels \\
\code{"sequential"}, \code{"consecutive"} & Each level versus the preceding level \\
\code{"reference"} & Every other level versus a reference \\
\code{"trt.vs.ctrl"} & Every treatment versus the control level \\
\code{"deviation"}, \code{"effect"} & Each level versus the equal-weight level mean \\
\code{"helmert"} & Successive Helmert comparisons \\
\code{"poly"} & Linear and higher polynomial patterns across levels \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\caption{Built-in comparison recipes. Aliases on the same row are
equivalent.}
\label{tab:recipes}
\end{table}

There are no reverse variants of these recipes. Reversing a comparison
does not change its inertia, pseudo-$F$, or permutation $p$-value. When
the displayed direction is important, write the desired character
comparison explicitly.

For example, all management types can be compared with BF:

<<reference>>=
comparisons(mod, "Management", comparison = "reference",
            reference = "BF", permutations = perm, parallel = 1)
@

If the wording of the study identifies a control, \code{"trt.vs.ctrl"}
expresses the same family of questions. By default, the first fitted
factor level is the control. Use \code{relevel} before fitting to change
the factor's reference level, or supply \code{reference} to override it
for this call. BF is already the first level of \code{Management}, so no
override is needed here.

<<control>>=
comparisons(mod, "Management", comparison = "trt.vs.ctrl",
            permutations = perm, parallel = 1)
@

Consecutive comparisons are useful when levels have a meaningful
ordering.

<<consecutive>>=
comparisons(mod, "Management", comparison = "sequential",
            permutations = perm, parallel = 1)
@

Helmert and polynomial recipes are available from base R. Polynomial
comparisons are usually meaningful only when the factor levels really
describe an ordered progression. They operate on equally spaced level
positions; they do not infer numerical spacing from level labels.

\section{Writing specific comparisons}

Specific scientific questions can be written directly as character
strings. Factor-level names stand for their fitted multivariate
scenarios. The right-hand side must currently be zero.

<<specific>>=
specific <- comparisons(
    mod, "Management",
    comparison = c(
        "HF - BF = 0",
        "(NM + SF) / 2 - BF = 0"
    ),
    permutations = perm, parallel = 1)
specific
@

The second row compares BF with the average of NM and SF. Parentheses,
addition, subtraction, unary signs, and multiplication or division by
numeric constants are supported. A level name containing spaces or
operator characters must be enclosed in backticks. Expressions are
parsed by a restricted parser: R functions cannot be called from them.

Names can make the output easier to read.

<<named-specific>>=
comparisons(
    mod, "Management",
    comparison = c(
        HF_vs_BF = "HF - BF = 0",
        managed_mean_vs_BF = "(NM + SF) / 2 - BF = 0"
    ),
    permutations = perm, parallel = 1)
@

Programmatic code can instead supply weights. A named numeric vector
defines one comparison; a matrix defines one comparison per row. There
must be one weight for every fitted factor level, and the weights in
each row must sum to zero.

<<numeric-comparisons>>=
cmat <- rbind(
    HF_vs_BF = c(BF = -1, HF = 1, NM = 0, SF = 0),
    managed_mean_vs_BF = c(BF = -1, HF = 0, NM = 0.5, SF = 0.5)
)
comparisons(mod, "Management", comparison = cmat,
            permutations = perm, parallel = 1)
@

A named list may mix individual character and numeric specifications.
This can be convenient when a program constructs the comparisons one at
a time.

<<list-comparisons>>=
clist <- list(
    HF_vs_BF = "HF - BF = 0",
    SF_vs_NM = c(BF = 0, HF = 0, NM = -1, SF = 1)
)
comparisons(mod, "Management", comparison = clist,
            permutations = perm, parallel = 1)
@

\section{Combining comparisons and joint tests}

The \code{hypothesis} argument combines comparisons that were generated
in a first step. Names \code{b1}, \code{b2}, and so on refer to those
rows in their displayed order. For example, with BF as the reference,
the first two rows are HF--BF and NM--BF. Their difference is therefore
NM--HF:

<<composed>>=
comparisons(mod, "Management", comparison = "reference",
            reference = "BF", hypothesis = "b2 - b1 = 0",
            permutations = perm, parallel = 1)
@

This is a new comparison test. The function combines the underlying
fitted scenarios and then calculates a fresh inertia and pseudo-$F$; it
does not subtract two pseudo-$F$ statistics or two $p$-values. Named
first-stage comparisons can also be used in the expression.

Longer linear combinations use the same notation. For example, the
following combines the first three rows of the pairwise set:

<<general-hypothesis>>=
comparisons(mod, "Management", comparison = "pairwise",
            hypothesis = "b1 + b2 - b3 = 0",
            permutations = perm, parallel = 1)
@

The row numbers always refer to the generated comparisons, not to model
coefficients. Inspecting the first-stage result before composing a
hypothesis makes the intended question explicit.

Several comparison rows can be tested together with \code{joint = TRUE}.
The result is one multi-degree-of-freedom test of the whole set.

<<joint>>=
comparisons(mod, "Management", comparison = "trt.vs.ctrl",
            reference = "BF", joint = TRUE,
            permutations = perm, parallel = 1)
@

A joint test asks whether any independent dimension in the set is
present. It does not identify which row is responsible. Redundant rows
are reduced to their independent rank automatically.

When separate tests are retained, their $p$-values can be adjusted with
any method accepted by \code{p.adjust}. For example:

<<adjust>>=
comparisons(mod, "Management", comparison = "pairwise",
            adjust = "holm", permutations = perm, parallel = 1)
@

Adjustment changes only the reported multiplicity correction. It does
not change the individual pseudo-$F$ tests. A common maximum-statistic
adjustment is not provided because the separate comparisons generally
have different null models.

\section{Interactions and simple effects}

When the model contains an interaction, a difference between treatment
levels may depend on the interacting variable. We use a small balanced
example with three treatments in two habitats. The response has six
species, and blocks will later be used to restrict permutations.

<<interaction-data>>=
set.seed(42)
idat <- expand.grid(
    block = factor(seq_len(6)),
    habitat = factor(c("grass", "heath")),
    treatment = factor(c("control", "low", "high"),
                       levels = c("control", "low", "high"))
)
iY <- matrix(rpois(nrow(idat) * 6, lambda = 5), ncol = 6)
iY[, 1] <- iY[, 1] + 3 * (idat$treatment == "high")
iY[, 2] <- iY[, 2] + 3 * (idat$habitat == "heath")
iY[, 3] <- iY[, 3] +
    4 * (idat$treatment == "high" & idat$habitat == "heath")
imod <- rda(iY ~ treatment * habitat, data = idat)

set.seed(314)
iper <- shuffleSet(nrow(idat), nset = 99,
                   control = how(blocks = idat$block))
@

By default, \code{newdata = NULL} makes a counterfactual copy of every
analysis row at every treatment level and averages the resulting model
rows. The other variables retain their observed distribution. The
following result is therefore an average treatment comparison over the
observed habitats:

<<interaction-average>>=
comparisons(imod, "treatment", comparison = "trt.vs.ctrl",
            reference = "control", permutations = iper, parallel = 1)
@

The \code{by} argument instead calculates treatment comparisons within
each habitat. These are often called simple effects.

<<interaction-by>>=
iby <- comparisons(imod, "treatment", comparison = "trt.vs.ctrl",
                   reference = "control", by = "habitat",
                   permutations = iper, parallel = 1)
iby
@

More than one variable may be named in \code{by}. Each observed
combination becomes a group.

An explicit \code{newdata} data frame asks about a chosen population or
setting. Here treatment comparisons are evaluated only in heath. The
factor is created with the levels used in the fitted model.

<<interaction-newdata>>=
heath <- data.frame(
    habitat = factor("heath", levels = levels(idat$habitat))
)
comparisons(imod, "treatment", comparison = "trt.vs.ctrl",
            reference = "control", newdata = heath,
            permutations = iper, parallel = 1)
@

Supplying several rows in \code{newdata} averages over those rows; it
does not automatically produce a separate result for each row. Use
\code{by} when separate results are wanted.

The \code{hypothesis} argument can compare simple effects between
groups. The next call first generates high--control within each habitat;
its \code{b1} and \code{b2} rows are then compared. This is a
difference-in-differences test:

<<difference-in-differences>>=
comparisons(imod, "treatment", comparison = "high - control = 0",
            by = "habitat", hypothesis = "b2 - b1 = 0",
            permutations = iper, parallel = 1)
@

When \code{by} and \code{hypothesis} are used together, the \code{b}
indices cover all grouped rows in their printed order. It is often
safest to first run the grouped comparison, inspect its row order, and
then write the second-stage hypothesis.

The same approach extends to higher-order interactions: include all
moderators needed to identify the desired slice in \code{by} or
\code{newdata}, then compare the resulting simple effects.

\section{Choosing the averaging population}

The meaning of a comparison in a model with other predictors depends on
the values over which those predictors are averaged. There are three
main choices:

\begin{description}
  \item[\code{newdata = NULL}] Average counterfactual model rows over
    the fitted analysis observations. This is the default and reflects
    the observed mix of covariates and factor levels.
  \item[\code{newdata = "balanced"}] Give every combination of
    non-focal factor levels equal representation, with numeric variables
    fixed at their means. This describes a balanced reference grid
    rather than the observed sample composition.
  \item[\code{newdata = data.frame(...)}] Average over a population or
    settings supplied by the user. It must contain the variables needed
    to reconstruct the fitted model terms, apart from the focal factor,
    which \code{comparisons} supplies in turn.
\end{description}

For the balanced interaction example, the empirical and balanced
averages coincide closely because both habitats have equal
representation. The syntax is:

<<balanced-grid>>=
comparisons(imod, "treatment", comparison = "high - control = 0",
            newdata = "balanced", permutations = iper, parallel = 1)
@

Use \code{wts} when rows should contribute unequally. It can be a
non-negative numeric vector or the name of a column in \code{newdata}.
Weights are normalized within each \code{by} group. In this artificial
example, heath observations receive twice the averaging weight of grass
observations:

<<weighted>>=
ipop <- idat[!duplicated(idat[c("block", "habitat")]),
             c("block", "habitat")]
ipop$sampling_weight <- ifelse(ipop$habitat == "heath", 2, 1)
comparisons(imod, "treatment", comparison = "high - control = 0",
            newdata = ipop, wts = "sampling_weight",
            permutations = iper, parallel = 1)
@

The weights control only averaging of fitted scenarios. They are not
observation weights in the fitted ordination model.

Numeric values outside the range of the fitted data are allowed but are
flagged as extrapolations in the result:

<<extrapolation>>=
xmod <- rda(dune ~ Management * A1, data = dune.env)
xcmp <- comparisons(
    xmod, "Management", comparison = "HF - BF = 0",
    newdata = data.frame(A1 = 10), permutations = perm, parallel = 1)
attr(xcmp, "extrapolation")
@

Such a result may answer a mathematically defined question while having
little empirical support, so it should be interpreted cautiously.

\section{Conditions and permutation designs}

Original \code{Condition} terms are retained in every comparison test.
Here moisture is partialled out before management comparisons are
tested in correspondence analysis:

<<cca-condition>>=
cmod <- cca(dune ~ Management + Condition(Moisture), data = dune.env)
comparisons(cmod, "Management", comparison = "reference",
            reference = "BF", permutations = perm, parallel = 1)
@

Permutation restrictions can be supplied in any form accepted by
vegan's permutation infrastructure. A predefined permutation matrix,
as used throughout this vignette, is particularly useful because it is
reused for all rows. Alternatively, \code{strata} restricts
permutations to blocks:

<<strata-example,eval=false>>=
comparisons(imod, "treatment", comparison = "trt.vs.ctrl",
            reference = "control", permutations = 99,
            strata = idat$block)
@

The \code{parallel} argument is passed to \code{permutest.cca}. At
present, comparison rows are processed sequentially and the
permutations within each row are parallelized. An integer gives the
number of workers; an existing socket cluster may also be supplied.
Using \code{parallel = 1}, as in this vignette, avoids parallel startup
overhead for these deliberately small tests.

\section{CCA, dbRDA, and PERMANOVA}

The user interface is shared by RDA, CCA, and distance-based RDA. The
meaning of inertia follows the fitted method: variance for RDA,
chi-square inertia for CCA, and squared-distance inertia for dbRDA. CCA
also uses its weighted chi-square geometry in constructing the
comparison test.

<<dbrda>>=
dmod <- dbrda(dune ~ Management + A1, data = dune.env,
              distance = "bray")
comparisons(dmod, "Management", comparison = "reference",
            reference = "BF", permutations = perm, parallel = 1)
@

For PERMANOVA, the default return value of \code{adonis2} remains its
ANOVA table. Request \code{return = "model"} when post-fit comparisons
will be needed:

<<adonis-model>>=
afit <- adonis2(dune ~ Management + A1, data = dune.env,
                method = "bray", return = "model")
comparisons(afit, "Management", comparison = "trt.vs.ctrl",
            reference = "BF", permutations = perm, parallel = 1)
@

Retaining the model is opt-in because it requires more information to
remain in memory than the usual \code{adonis2} table. For non-Euclidean
dissimilarities, negative eigenvalues retain their existing dbRDA
interpretation; the comparison output should not be described as a
response-vector effect size.

\section{Using the ANOVA interface}

Code organized around \code{anova} can request \code{by = "contrast"}.
This is a thin interface to the same compiler and testing code:

<<anova-bridge>>=
anova(mod, by = "contrast", variables = "Management",
      comparison = "reference", reference = "BF",
      permutations = perm, parallel = 1)
@

The result uses the familiar \code{anova.cca} layout. The full
\code{comparisons} interface is preferable when reference populations,
grouped comparisons, composed hypotheses, or multiplicity adjustments
are needed. Existing \code{by = "onedf"} tests have a different
purpose: they are sequential tests of the columns produced by the
factor coding and do not define coding-invariant scientific
comparisons.

\section{Limitations and useful checks}

The initial interface intentionally has a narrow boundary:

\begin{itemize}
  \item exactly one focal factor is named in \code{variables};
  \item the model must have been fitted with the formula interface and
    must have a full-rank constraint matrix;
  \item character expressions must be linear and have zero on the
    right-hand side;
  \item ratios between fitted levels, nonlinear functions,
    inequalities, equivalence regions, and non-zero null values are not
    supported;
  \item factor levels and interaction cells needed for a comparison
    must be represented well enough for the comparison to be estimable;
  \item separate tests use separate contrast-specific null models, even
    though they share a permutation matrix.
\end{itemize}

The returned object retains information useful for checking an
analysis. For example:

<<attributes>>=
names(attributes(pw))
attr(pw, "control")
attr(pw, "extrapolation")
@

The \code{comparison.matrices} attribute contains the reference rows and
compiled hypotheses. These are mainly intended for diagnostics and
method development; most users should define questions with the public
arguments rather than modifying these matrices.

\appendix
\section{Technical implementation}

This appendix describes why the tests are invariant to the coding of a
factor and how a requested comparison is turned into a pseudo-$F$
test. Coefficients appear here only as an internal coordinate system.
They are not the reported estimands or test statistics.

\subsection{From reference rows to a hypothesis}

Let $X$ be the $n \times p$ constraint matrix of the fitted model,
after vegan's usual handling of its intercept and centering. For every
level of the focal factor, \code{comparisons} constructs counterfactual
data, rebuilds the model matrix using the fitted terms, factor levels,
and contrast coding, and averages its rows over the requested
population. Put the resulting level-specific rows into a matrix $R$.
Because the complete model matrix is rebuilt, interactions and
transformations enter $R$ exactly as they entered the fitted model.

A built-in recipe, expression, or numeric specification produces a
matrix $A$ of weights on the factor levels. Ordinary comparison rows
sum to zero. The requested hypothesis in the coordinates of the fitted
constraint space is
\[
  L = AR.
\]
If a second-stage \code{hypothesis} is supplied, its weight matrix $B$
is applied first, giving $L = BAR$. In particular, \code{b1},
\code{b2}, and so on identify rows of $AR$. This composition happens
before any inertia or test statistic is calculated.

Changing the contrast coding of the original factor changes the
coordinates in $R$ and $X$ together, but not the scientific scenario
difference or the subspace ultimately tested. This is why the result
does not depend on whether treatment, sum, or another full-rank coding
was used.

\subsection{The tested and null spaces}

Suppose $L$ has rank $q$. A complete QR decomposition of $L^\mathsf{T}$
provides an orthonormal basis
\[
  Q = [M\;N],
\]
where the $q$ columns of $M$ span the tested directions and the columns
of $N$ span their null-space complement. The fitted constraint space
is reparameterized as
\[
  X_1 = XM, \qquad X_0 = XN.
\]
The temporary constrained ordination uses $X_1$ as its constraints and
conditions on both $X_0$ and any original condition matrix $Z$:
\[
  Y \sim X_1 + \operatorname{Condition}(Z, X_0).
\]
Thus all fitted-model directions allowed under the comparison null are
retained rather than discarded. Adding $X_0$ and $X_1$ recovers the
original fitted constraint space, so the residual inertia and residual
degrees of freedom are those of the full fitted model.

The observed statistic is
\[
  F = \frac{I_L/q}{I_E/df_E},
\]
where $I_L$ is the additional constrained inertia assigned to the
hypothesis and $I_E$ and $df_E$ are the full-model residual inertia and
degrees of freedom. For a one-response RDA this reduces to the usual
partial $F$ test. For multivariate RDA it uses variance, for CCA it
uses weighted chi-square inertia, and for dbRDA it uses the geometry of
the fitted distance representation.

\subsection{Permutation test}

The temporary object is passed to \code{permutest.cca} with
\code{model = "reduced"}. In each permutation, residuals under the
conditioned comparison-null model are permuted and the pseudo-$F$ is
recalculated. The permutation $p$-value is
\[
  p = \frac{1 + \#\{F^{(b)} \geq F_{obs}\}}{1 + B},
\]
where $B$ is the number of permutations. One validated permutation
matrix is reused across requested rows, but each row is fitted and
tested separately because its null space can differ. With
\code{joint = TRUE}, rows are stacked, reduced to rank $q$, and tested
as one hypothesis.

This construction explains two deliberate interface choices. First,
a second-stage hypothesis must combine the underlying design-space
rows, not already calculated inertias or pseudo-$F$ values. Second,
only linear zero-null hypotheses are currently admitted: they identify
a constrained subspace with a direct partial-inertia pseudo-$F$ test.

\end{document}
